@article{Carlip17,
  author  = {Carlip, Steven},
  title   = {Dimension and dimensional reduction in quantum gravity},
  year    = {2017},
  note    = {Read in full by an assistant agent on 2026-10-23 from the arXiv text (arXiv:1705.05417v2, 29 May 2017, the PDF, all 34 pages); reading note docs/reading/notes/2026-10-23_carlip_dimensional_reduction.md. Journal reference Class. Quantum Grav. 34 (2017) 193001, DOI 10.1088/1361-6382/aa8535. The paper is a review of dimensional estimators and of hints that the effective dimension falls to about 2 at short distances; it does not claim that the fundamental degrees of freedom are two-dimensional, contains no holographic argument, and never discusses the degree of a graph. The general-knowledge remark below about Newton's constant being dimensionless at d = 2 is confirmed by its Secs. 3.3 and 4.1. Earlier note, written before the reading: A review rather than a single result, which is why it is worth the time: several unrelated approaches to quantum gravity converge on an effective dimension falling towards about 2 at short distances. Bears directly on whether "how many dimensions" is a quantity that can change with scale, which VISION's dimension ladder assumes it is. **Why it matters beyond bookkeeping** (ours, unverified, from general knowledge): the gravitational coupling has dimensions of length^(d-2), so it is dimensionless at d = 2 and carries units of area at d = 4 -- and it is that dimensionful coupling which makes general relativity non-renormalisable. If the effective dimension really falls to 2 at short distances, the obstruction disappears exactly where it bites. That is the usual motivation for caring about this result, and it is also the reason a two-dimensional starting point may be less arbitrary than it looks. Neither [T25] nor [KTB19] makes that connection in what we have read; one for the letter.}
}

